  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=5,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-2$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(3)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =5(3)+C \\\\ -2 & =15+C \\\\ C & =-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=5t-17.\n$$",
